od, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Chapter 9 — Aquinas's metaphysics of essence and existence, and the five ways#{2}":[49,49],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Chapter 9 — Aquinas's metaphysics of essence and existence, and the five ways#{3}":[50,50],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Chapter 9 — Aquinas's metaphysics of essence and existence, and the five ways#{4}":[51,51],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Chapter 9 — Aquinas's metaphysics of essence and existence, and the five ways#{5}":[52,52],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Chapter 9 — Aquinas's metaphysics of essence and existence, and the five ways#{6}":[53,54],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Chapter 9 — Soul, body, and personal identity":[55,59],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Chapter 9 — Soul, body, and personal identity#{1}":[57,57],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Chapter 9 — Soul, body, and personal identity#{2}":[58,59],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Chapter 10 — Natural law, virtue, and the life of practice":[60,68],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Chapter 10 — Natural law, virtue, and the life of practice#{1}":[62,62],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Chapter 10 — Natural law, virtue, and the life of practice#{2}":[63,63],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Chapter 10 — Natural law, virtue, and the life of practice#{3}":[64,64],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Chapter 10 — Natural law, virtue, and the life of practice#{4}":[65,65],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Chapter 10 — Natural law, virtue, and the life of practice#{5}":[66,66],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Chapter 10 — Natural law, virtue, and the life of practice#{6}":[67,68],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Chapter 11 — Aquinas's philosophy of university education":[69,74],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Chapter 11 — Aquinas's philosophy of university education#{1}":[71,71],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Chapter 11 — Aquinas's philosophy of university education#{2}":[72,72],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Chapter 11 — Aquinas's philosophy of university education#{3}":[73,74],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Bridge into Chapter 12 (context for the univocity/analogy debate)":[75,78],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Bridge into Chapter 12 (context for the univocity/analogy debate)#{1}":[77,78],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Pages updated from this source":[79,83],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Pages updated from this source#{1}":[81,81],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Pages updated from this source#{2}":[82,83],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Flagged for follow-up deep ingest":[84,87],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Flagged for follow-up deep ingest#{1}":[86,87],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Raw File":[88,91],"#Source: God, Philosophy, Universities: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition#Raw File#{1}":[90,91]},"outlinks":[{"title":"Alasdair MacIntyre","target":"Alasdair MacIntyre","line":14},{"title":"San Tommaso d'Aquino","target":"San Tommaso d'Aquino","line":15},{"title":"Tomismo","target":"Tomismo","line":16},{"title":"Dottrina di Atto e Potenza","target":"Dottrina di Atto e Potenza","line":17},{"title":"dopo-la-virtu","target":"dopo-la-virtu","line":18},{"title":"Dottrina di Atto e Potenza","target":"Dottrina di Atto e Potenza","line":77},{"title":"Tomismo","target":"Tomismo","line":77},{"title":"San Tommaso d'Aquino","target":"San Tommaso d'Aquino","line":81},{"title":"Dottrina di Atto e Potenza","target":"Dottrina di Atto e Potenza","line":82}],"metadata":{"type":"source","title":"God, Philosophy, Universities","address":"c-000028","created":"2026-07-25","updated":"2026-07-25","tags":["#tomismo","#etica","#source","#book"],"status":"developing","related":["[[Alasdair MacIntyre]]","[[San Tommaso d'Aquino]]","[[Tomismo]]","[[Dottrina di Atto e Potenza]]","[[dopo-la-virtu]]"],"source_type":"book","author":"Alasdair MacIntyre","date_published":"2009 (Rowman & Littlefield, subtitle: A Selective History of the Catholic Philosophical Tradition)","url":"","confidence":"high","key_claims":["Aquinas's central philosophical achievement is the real distinction between essence (essentia) and the act of existing (esse) in every finite being, culminating in God as ipsum esse subsistens — a distinction that lets Aquinas go beyond Aristotle while remaining Aristotelian.","Aquinas's account of the soul as substantial form of the body (not a Platonic separable substance) makes personal identity depend on the soul's relation to its particular body, which is why Aquinas holds resurrection of the body — not just survival of an immaterial soul — is needed for personal immortality.","Natural law's primary precepts are exactly the rules presupposed by the possibility of genuine rational deliberation among human beings as such (prohibiting killing the innocent, theft, lying, promise-breaking); this is why Aquinas holds they are the same for everyone, everywhere, while secondary precepts (their application) vary by circumstance.","University curriculum order (logic → mathematics → natural science → moral/political philosophy → metaphysics/theology) is not administrative convenience but follows the order in which human rational powers must actually develop toward the final end of perfected understanding."],"raw_file":".raw/A. MacIntyre - God, Philosophy, Universities.pdf"},"task_lines":[],"tasks":{},"codeblock_ranges":[]},"smart_blocks:Wiki/sources/god-philosophy-universities.md#---frontmatter---": {"path":null,"last_embed":{"hash":"ln3yn6","at":1785014066373},"embeddings":{"TaylorAI/bge-micro-v2":{"vec":[-0.02910574,-0.0055141,0.0138977,-0.02366264,-0.03053114,-0.001795,-0.00449302,0.02829267,0.03186005,0.04201942,-0.04912679,-0.05095381,0.00595397,0.01416211,0.0887167,-0.01581313,-0.01285082,0.0643599,-0.10095944,-0.00788679,0.10358981,-0.04610272,-0.01904266,-0.03648271,0.02047829,0.02935952,0.02481804,-0.06244537,-0.02753838,-0.16626206,-0.03388394,0.0193972,0.01468848,-0.01034743,-0.00666822,0.02228924,-0.01880288,-0.0095342,-0.02495797,0.04714026,0.03290997,0.08515462,-0.01366574,0.00119732,0.00067954,0.03855032,-0.01413307,0.01258228,-0.0392254,-0.06426825,-0.0382155,0.08562373,-0.03830299,0.01593183,-0.04001107,0.043017